1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,480
A checkout redesign's scope is knowable in advance.

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:08,573
Nobody needs to discover whether it's technically possible to add a shipping field.

3
00:00:08,573 --> 00:00:13,728
An AI feature is different in one specific way: whether ninety-five percent accuracy

4
00:00:13,728 --> 00:00:18,821
is achievable for this task is a genuine unknown until someone's actually built the

5
00:00:18,821 --> 00:00:23,853
eval and run it, and a roadmap that puts a firm date on that unknown is committing

6
00:00:23,853 --> 00:00:25,939
to a number nobody has earned yet.

7
00:00:25,939 --> 00:00:29,682
That's the lie in the title, and it's rarely told on purpose.

8
00:00:29,682 --> 00:00:34,101
It's told by a template that has one column for "when" and no column for

9
00:00:34,101 --> 00:00:39,071
"validated," inherited from years of roadmaps for features that never needed that

10
00:00:39,071 --> 00:00:40,360
second column at all.

11
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:45,208
Notice this isn't an argument against roadmaps, or against giving dates at all.

12
00:00:45,208 --> 00:00:50,117
Plenty of AI roadmap items genuinely are knowable in advance, once the discovery

13
00:00:50,117 --> 00:00:53,431
sprint's already run and the threshold's already real.

14
00:00:53,431 --> 00:00:55,701
The problem is never the date itself.

15
00:00:55,701 --> 00:01:00,242
It's a date that arrived before the evidence that should have produced it.

16
00:01:00,792 --> 00:01:05,900
Notice the good column doesn't refuse to commit, it just commits to the right thing

17
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,007
at the right time.

18
00:01:07,007 --> 00:01:11,131
A discovery sprint by a specific date is a real, checkable promise.

19
00:01:11,131 --> 00:01:16,300
A ship date and an accuracy number for a capability nobody's measured is two guesses

20
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:20,792
stacked on top of each other, presented as one confident line on a slide.

21
00:01:20,792 --> 00:01:25,962
Notice, too, that the evidence-first line isn't vaguer, it's just honest about which

22
00:01:25,962 --> 00:01:27,192
part is known today.

23
00:01:27,192 --> 00:01:32,239
"Discovery sprint by March" is exactly as specific a commitment as "ships Q3." The

24
00:01:32,239 --> 00:01:36,608
only difference is which fact it's actually willing to promise, and the

25
00:01:36,608 --> 00:01:40,793
evidence-first version never promises a number it hasn't earned yet.

26
00:01:40,793 --> 00:01:45,901
The bad column's Q3 conversation is the expensive one, and it's entirely avoidable.

27
00:01:45,901 --> 00:01:50,701
Nobody enjoys explaining why a promised ninety-five percent arrived at eighty.

28
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:55,747
Almost everybody accepts "we're running the sprint in March, and we'll have a real

29
00:01:55,747 --> 00:02:00,916
number and a real date by April" without complaint, because it's honest about what's

30
00:02:00,916 --> 00:02:02,455
actually known right now.

31
00:02:03,005 --> 00:02:07,826
Sit with that meeting specifically, because it's the one every date-first roadmap is

32
00:02:07,826 --> 00:02:10,983
quietly scheduling months in advance without naming it.

33
00:02:11,533 --> 00:02:16,417
Read the order left to right, because it's the actual sequence, not just a table

34
00:02:16,417 --> 00:02:16,844
layout.

35
00:02:16,844 --> 00:02:21,972
What happens if you fill the date column before the threshold column has anything in

36
00:02:21,972 --> 00:02:22,155
it?

37
00:02:22,155 --> 00:02:27,283
Exactly the trap this lecture opened with, just formatted differently: sprint status

38
00:02:27,283 --> 00:02:31,374
before threshold, threshold before date, never the other way round.

39
00:02:31,374 --> 00:02:36,379
This table is also where 9.1's metrics stop being abstract and become the specific

40
00:02:36,379 --> 00:02:38,089
number a roadmap answers to.

41
00:02:38,089 --> 00:02:42,728
The threshold column isn't "good performance," it's adoption, acceptance, or

42
00:02:42,728 --> 00:02:47,856
deflection, at a number someone measured, the same discipline the whole back half of

43
00:02:47,856 --> 00:02:51,458
this course has been building toward one section at a time.

44
00:02:51,458 --> 00:02:56,403
Notice, too, that this table doesn't ask for more total information than a normal

45
00:02:56,403 --> 00:03:01,042
roadmap line, just the same information in an order that makes an unmeasured

46
00:03:01,042 --> 00:03:03,301
commitment visible instead of hidden.

47
00:03:03,301 --> 00:03:08,246
A line with "in progress" in the sprint-status column and nothing in threshold is

48
00:03:08,246 --> 00:03:13,374
honest and incomplete, which is a completely different, much safer state than a line

49
00:03:13,374 --> 00:03:16,609
with a confident-looking date and an invented number.

50
00:03:17,159 --> 00:03:22,336
Say the honest caveat, because pretending this trade-off doesn't exist helps nobody

51
00:03:22,336 --> 00:03:25,330
walking into a real roadmap review next quarter.

52
00:03:25,330 --> 00:03:30,320
A roadmap full of specific dates reads as more confident than one with "date set

53
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:35,372
after the sprint" next to half its rows, even when the first one is substantially

54
00:03:35,372 --> 00:03:35,996
less true.

55
00:03:35,996 --> 00:03:41,048
This is 10.2's calibration principle again, aimed at an entire quarter instead of

56
00:03:41,048 --> 00:03:46,038
one stakeholder conversation: the honest version costs you something in the room

57
00:03:46,038 --> 00:03:50,841
today, and pays you back the day a date would otherwise have quietly slipped.

58
00:03:50,841 --> 00:03:55,643
Resist the urge to solve this by quietly filling the threshold column with an

59
00:03:55,643 --> 00:03:58,887
optimistic guess just to make the row look finished.

60
00:03:58,887 --> 00:04:03,752
That's the exact failure this lecture is naming, now hiding one column deeper.

61
00:04:03,752 --> 00:04:06,434
An empty threshold cell is an honest state.

62
00:04:06,434 --> 00:04:11,112
A guessed one dressed up as measured is the same hedge from 10.2, wearing a

63
00:04:11,112 --> 00:04:13,233
spreadsheet instead of a sentence.

64
00:04:13,783 --> 00:04:18,643
That's the standard worth holding the next roadmap review to, and the exercise is

65
00:04:18,643 --> 00:04:23,204
worth running on the actual document your team uses, not a hypothetical one.

66
00:04:23,204 --> 00:04:27,944
Most roadmaps, checked honestly, have at least one line where the answer is no,

67
00:04:27,944 --> 00:04:32,865
neither number was measured, it was estimated once in a planning meeting and never

68
00:04:32,865 --> 00:04:33,465
revisited.

69
00:04:33,465 --> 00:04:38,385
That line is this lecture's finding, and it's usually the line worth fixing before

70
00:04:38,385 --> 00:04:41,926
the next review, not the one furthest away on the timeline.

71
00:04:41,926 --> 00:04:46,246
Run this on the item that worries you most, not the safest one to check.

72
00:04:46,246 --> 00:04:50,867
The feature everyone's already excited about, the one already mentioned in an

73
00:04:50,867 --> 00:04:55,667
external announcement, is exactly the one most likely to be carrying an invented

74
00:04:55,667 --> 00:04:58,427
number nobody's had the nerve to question yet.

75
00:04:58,977 --> 00:05:00,728
One thing before you move on.

76
00:05:00,728 --> 00:05:02,298
Add the two columns first.

77
00:05:02,298 --> 00:05:06,947
It's a small template change that makes the gap this lecture named visible to

78
00:05:06,947 --> 00:05:09,423
everyone who reads the roadmap after you.

79
00:05:09,423 --> 00:05:14,314
Then find the one dated item that's honestly still a guess, and say so plainly at

80
00:05:14,314 --> 00:05:17,997
the next review, before the date arrives and says it for you.

81
00:05:17,997 --> 00:05:21,318
Neither of these requires anyone's permission to start.

82
00:05:21,318 --> 00:05:26,269
A template column and one honest flag are both things you can do this week, on the

83
00:05:26,269 --> 00:05:30,918
roadmap you already have, before the next review makes the gap someone else's

84
00:05:30,918 --> 00:05:32,125
problem to discover.

85
00:05:32,125 --> 00:05:37,077
Next lecture is vibe-coding your own tools: when building something small yourself

86
00:05:37,077 --> 00:05:40,699
is the right call, and what it actually costs when it isn't.
